  "title": "A note: counting homomorphisms from knot groups to solvable groups can be #P-hard",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "A self-contained 14-page note on Rowell's Conjecture 3.2(b) (2009), with replay and review package. On links, computing |Hom(link group, D_n)| is #P-hard for every odd n >= 3, including S3 (new here: local torus knots isolate the rotation sector and 2-cables weight it, giving n^(2m) Z_Gamma(n, 1)); with boundedly many components metabelian targets are polynomial. On knots, where metabelian targets are polynomial, counting is #P-hard under polynomial-time Turing reductions for S4, GL(2,3) and 2O (S4 is the smallest such group), for UT3(F4) x| C3 (knot-group count = 3-surgery count + 63), and for H(F_p) x| C_r (p odd) exactly when a, b and ab are nontrivial, with the polynomial converse also for bounded first homology. So Conjecture 3.2(b) fails unless every #P function is polynomial-time computable. For UT3(F4) x| C3 the reduction preserves approximation, so approximating knot-group counts is #BIS-hard. The knot theorems restate the cited repository proofs with the follow-up review's qualifications.",
